nodecode-openrouter-spend is a rewrite of AllenRobinHubert's AllenRobinHubert/openrouter-spend:

  https://github.com/AllenRobinHubert/openrouter-spend
  commit 4a54b43f82264a0703d21abec9f7e49e8650abcf
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Allen Robin Hubert (LICENSE)

The original is a Hermes desktop plugin: a status-bar chip and a popup page
over a FastAPI backend, a /spend command, and Python that read OpenRouter's
accounting with the key from Hermes's own profile, kept the counters in
SQLite per key, showed them in USD and INR, and measured the month against a
configurable baseline. What was rewritten: the one accounting read the
plugin makes itself -- GET openrouter.ai/api/v1/key with the key in one
Authorization header, and the plugin's whitelist of the six numeric fields
(usage, usage_daily, usage_weekly, usage_monthly, limit, limit_remaining)
plus byok_usage -- its refusals for a rejected key and an answer with no
counters, its USD/INR reference rate (api.frankfurter.dev, validated against
base/quote and a ceiling, with a manual override and a cached fallback), its
per-key cache of the last good reading, its month-against-a-baseline line,
and its rule that a key's label can contain part of the key, so the record
is filed under a hash instead. The plugin's configuration surface (which
variable holds the key, the two cache windows, the timeout, the manual rate,
the comparison) is the cell's :section; the chip, the popup page and the
FastAPI routes are one text report, shown by the openrouter_spend tool, by
/openrouter-spend and by (openrouter-spend:report), with
(openrouter-spend:history) answering the local record.

What was left out, and why: the per-request ledger the plugin fed from
Hermes's pre_api_request, api_request_error, post_auxiliary_call and
post_tool_call hooks and its llm_execution middleware -- per-request cost,
model, token counts and session -- and the /generation reconciliation that
resolved missing costs from the generation ids those hooks kept, because
Nodecode's cell contract has no point that hands a cell a provider response.
The local record is therefore one reading per fetch, and it shows what the
key spent between two of them. Also left out: the SQLite database and its
migrations/snapshots tables, replaced by one JSON file; the per-profile
fan-out over Hermes homes and the merged profile/model/token tables the
plugin's dashboard built from state.db; the Hermes secret-scope lookup and
the desktop plugin API (dashboard/plugin_api.py's /summary, /chat, /other and
their manual-usage rows); and the status-bar refresh timer, since a report is
a text the operator or the model asks for.
